remote-build.py gates a dirty working tree without noticing: an unresolved merge or half-written edit is rsynced as source, so conflict markers arrive at the compiler as syntax errors


## Found the hard way, 2026-09-01

Two sessions share this repo's main checkout. A peer began merging its lane while
this session ran `just rclippy-delta` on what it believed was its own post-merge
master. The gate rsynced the peer's **conflicted working tree** and returned:

    ADDED [(uncoded)] src/typecheck/checker.rs (new): encountered diff marker
    ADDED [(uncoded)] src/typecheck/checker.rs (new): expected at least one digit in exponent
    clippy checked zero workspace units, so it found nothing because it looked at
    nothing. An empty result is not a clean result.

**The gate refused correctly** — B083's zero-units guard did its job and stopped a
false green. The defect is the DIAGNOSIS, not the verdict.

## Why this is worth fixing rather than remembering

Nothing in that message says "another session is mid-merge in your checkout". It
reads as *your code does not compile*. **This is the third time in this batch
that an infrastructure condition has been reported as a code defect** — the other
two were a full disk on x-alpha (B106) and a name-filtered run printing a green
summary. Same shape every time: the tool reports what it saw rather than why it
could not see.

The mechanism, so it is not re-derived: **the remote gate does not and cannot see
the git index. It copies the FILES.** An unresolved merge, a half-written edit,
or any dirty tree is measured as source, and conflict markers arrive at the
compiler as syntax.

## Deliverable

A pre-sync cleanliness probe in `scripts/remote-build.py`:

1. If `.git/MERGE_HEAD` (or `REBASE_HEAD`, `CHERRY_PICK_HEAD`) exists, **refuse
   with a named error** — "the working tree has an unresolved merge; gating it
   would measure conflict markers, not your code."
2. Report unmerged paths from `git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U` in that
   message, so the user sees WHICH files.
3. Consider a softer warning for a merely dirty tree (`git status --porcelain`),
   since gating uncommitted work is legitimate and routine here. **Do not refuse
   on dirty** — only on an unresolved operation. Refusing on dirty would break
   every lane's normal workflow.

This converts the worst diagnostic in the system into the clearest.

## Test axes

- `MERGE_HEAD` present -> refuses, names the unmerged files.
- A rebase and a cherry-pick in progress -> also refuse (different sentinel
  files; do not test only the merge case).
- Dirty but no operation in progress -> **proceeds**, since that is the normal
  lane workflow and a refusal here would be a regression.
- Clean tree -> proceeds silently, no new output.
- **Mutation:** delete the check and confirm a tree with conflict markers reaches
  the remote. A guard that cannot be shown to fire is not a guard — see the
  hazards catalogue, where four of nine such guards were vacuous in one ticket.

## The coordination rule that saved this incident

Recorded in `docs/verification-hazards.md`: **in a shared checkout the index
belongs to whoever started the operation.** This session did not run
`merge --abort`, `checkout` or `reset` on a merge it did not start. Abort is not
a cleanup command when the mess is someone else's.